-- title:
--   Theorem 2.23 — Nash equilibria converge to the mean field equilibrium at rate $1/N$ (general case)
-- statement:
--   Let Assumptions 2.17, 2.19 and 2.20 hold. Suppose that $(m,\alpha)$ is a mean field equilibrium with state process $X$, and that for each $N$ there is a Nash equilibrium $\boldsymbol\alpha^N=(\alpha^{N,1},\dots,\alpha^{N,N})$ of the $N$-player game with state process $\mathbf X^N=(X^{N,1},\dots,X^{N,N})$. Let $(X^i)_{i=1,\dots,N}$ and $(\alpha^i)_{i=1,\dots,N}$ be the conditionally independent copies of $X$ and $\alpha$ (Definition 2.2). Then there is a constant $C$ independent of $N$ such that for each $N\in\mathbb N$ and $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$,
--   $$\mathbb E\Big[\sup_{0\le t\le T}|X^{N,i}_t-X^i_t|^2\Big]\le\frac CN.$$
--   Suppose in addition that the optimizer $\hat\alpha=\hat\alpha(x,y,z,z^0,m)$ satisfies
--
--   1. $\hat\alpha$ is Lipschitz;
--   2. for each fixed $(x,y,z,z^0)$, the map $m\mapsto\hat\alpha(x,y,z,z^0,m)$ admits derivatives $D_m\hat\alpha(x,y,z,z^0,m,p)$ and $D_{mm}\hat\alpha(x,y,z,z^0,m,p,q)$ which are bounded and Lipschitz continuous, uniformly in $(x,y,z,z^0)$.
--
--   Then there is a constant $C$ independent of $N$ such that for each $N$ and $i$,
--   $$\mathbb E\Big[\int_0^T|\alpha^i_t-\alpha^{N,i}_t|^2dt\Big]\le\frac CN.$$
--
--   This is a conditional propagation of chaos for open-loop Nash equilibria with controlled volatility and common noise: the $N$-player equilibrium states and controls are $C/N$-close in mean square to independent-given-$W^0$ copies of the mean field equilibrium.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the canonical spaces $\Omega^N=(\mathbb R^n)^N\times\mathcal C^{N+1}$ with product measure, embedded in one another (pp.9–10) → one probability space carrying $\xi^1,\xi^2,\dots$ (law $m_0$), $W^0$ and $W^1,W^2,\dots$, mutually independent, Brownian paths continuous (`Setup`); reason: the canonical product space is an instance, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - generalization: $d,k,n\in\mathbb N=\{1,2,\dots\}$ → any $n,k,d\ge 0$; reason: the source's dimensions are instances
--   - encoding: augmented filtrations $\mathbb F^N$, $\mathbb F^0$ → the raw filtrations generated by $\xi^1,\dots,\xi^N,W^0,\dots,W^N$ and by $W^0$; reason: every process progressive for the augmented filtration has a raw-progressive version ([41, Prop. 1.2.1], cited p.10); controls and states enter only up to versions
--   - encoding: continuous processes → processes with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$; reason: on a general probability space continuity holds outside a null set; suprema and integrals do not see null sets
--   - encoding: players $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ → players `i : Fin N`, player $i$ of the paper is index $i-1$; reason: relabelling
--   - encoding: $\mathbb R^{n\times d}$ with the Frobenius norm and $A\cdot B=\mathrm{tr}(A^\top B)$ → `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n × Fin d)`; reason: same norm and inner product
--   - encoding: a Nash equilibrium $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$ and "the state process corresponding to $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$" → `IsNashN`: admissible $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$, its state process $\mathbf X^N$ solving (2.3), compared against every admissible deviation and every state process of the deviation; reason: strong solutions are unique under Lipschitz data, so "every" and "the" agree
--   - encoding: $J^{N,i}$, $J_m$ real valued (footnote 1, p.12) → the costs of the equilibrium are finite, and only deviations with finite (integrable) cost are compared; reason: under the paper's assumptions every cost is finite, so the compared sets coincide
--   - encoding: $m_t=\mathcal L(X_t\mid\mathcal F^0_t)$ → $\omega\mapsto m_t(\omega)(A)$ is a.s. equal to $\mathbb P[X_t\in A\mid\mathcal F^0_t]$ for every Borel $A$ (`IsCondLaw`); $\mathbb F^0$-adaptedness of $m$ through the evaluation maps; reason: this is the definition of a version of the conditional law
--   - encoding: the copies $X^i_t=\widetilde X_t(\xi^i,W^0_{[0,t]},W^i_{[0,t]})$ of Definition 2.2 built from the representation of Lemma 2.1 → copies built from any representation (`ContRep` for $X$: copies adapted with a.s. continuous paths; `ProgRep` for $\alpha$: copies progressive); reason: Lemma 2.1 provides one, and two representations give a.s. equal copies at each $t$
--   - encoding: §2.3: Fréchet differentiability of the lift $\xi\mapsto U(x,\mathcal L(\xi))$ on $\mathbb L^2(\Omega)$, with continuous $D_mU$ → the published `DisplMonoMFG.WellPosed.HasLDeriv` (first-order expansion uniform over the joint laws of $(\xi,\eta)$ with $\mathcal L(\xi)=m$), plus joint continuity; versions of $D_m$ as data (`Derivs`); reason: on an atomless space the two notions coincide; a Lions derivative is determined only $m$-a.e., and every statement evaluates it on the support
--   - encoding: "Lipschitz in all arguments", "bounded", "$C^1$" for maps on $\mathbb R^p\times\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R^n)$ → `LipXM`, `BddXM`, `IsC1` with $\mathcal W_2$ in the measure and the max-norm on the product of the other arguments; reason: equivalent norms give the same notions up to the (existential) constants
--   - encoding: "for any square-integrable random variables" in (1.6), (2.16), (2.19) → for every coupling $\pi$ with finite second moments, the laws being the marginals of $\pi$, whenever the integrand is integrable; reason: random variables enter only through their joint law, and every coupling is a joint law on an atomless space
--   - encoding: $\mathbb E[\sup_t|\cdot|^2]$, $\mathbb E\int_0^T|\cdot|^2dt$ → lower Lebesgue integrals in $[0,\infty]$, bound $\mathrm{ofReal}(C/N)$; reason: same value; an infinite left side violates the bound in both
--   - reading: Assumption 2.20: "similarly, the functions $D_yH$, $D_zH$, and $D_{z^0}H$ are bounded and Lipschitz continuous, uniformly in $(x,y,z,z^0)$" → their first and second Lions derivatives in $m$ are bounded and Lipschitz in $m$, uniformly; reason: the sentence continues the description of the two Lions derivatives; the literal reading would add the hypothesis that $D_yH=b(x,\hat\alpha,m)$ is bounded
--   - reading: "bounded and Lipschitz in $m$, uniformly in $(x,y,z,z^0)$" (Assumption 2.20, Theorem 2.23 (2)) → one bound and one $\mathcal W_2$-Lipschitz constant in $m$, valid for all other arguments including the points $p,q$ (`TwoLionsUnif`); reason: the page asks Lipschitz continuity in $m$ uniformly in the remaining arguments
--   - encoding: "a constant $C$ independent of $N$" → $\exists C$ after the MFE, the family of equilibria and the representations, before $N$ and $i$; reason: $C$ may depend on everything except $N$ and $i$
--   - encoding: "for each $N$ there is a Nash equilibrium $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$" → a family $(\boldsymbol\alpha^N,\mathbf X^N)_{N\ge1}$ of Nash equilibria with their state processes; reason: the conclusion ranges over $N$
-- source:
--   Jackson & Tangpi, Quantitative Convergence for Displacement Monotone Mean Field Games with Controlled Volatility, arXiv:2304.04543v2, Theorem 2.23, pp.17–18; Definition 2.2 p.11

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CtrlVolMFG_IsSol_3_6

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace CtrlVolMFG

/-- Theorem 2.23 (Jackson & Tangpi, arXiv:2304.04543v2, pp.17–18): let Assumptions 2.17, 2.19 and
2.20 hold. Suppose that `(m, α)` is a MFE with state process `X`, and that for each `N` there is a
Nash equilibrium `αᴺ = (α^{N,1}, …, α^{N,N})` of the `N`-player game with state process
`Xᴺ = (X^{N,1}, …, X^{N,N})`. Let `(Xⁱ)`, `(αⁱ)` be conditionally independent copies of `X` and `α`
(Definition 2.2). Then there is a constant `C` independent of `N` such that for each `N ∈ ℕ` and
`i ∈ {1, …, N}`, `𝔼[sup_{0≤t≤T}|X^{N,i}_t − Xⁱ_t|²] ≤ C/N`. If in addition `α̂` is Lipschitz and,
for each fixed `(x, y, z, z⁰)`, `m ↦ α̂(x, y, z, z⁰, m)` admits derivatives `D_mα̂`, `D_mmα̂` which
are bounded and Lipschitz continuous uniformly in `(x, y, z, z⁰)`, then there is a constant `C`
independent of `N` such that `𝔼[∫₀ᵀ|αⁱ_t − α^{N,i}_t|²dt] ≤ C/N`.

**Formalization Note** All games live on one probability space (`Setup`; player `i` is index
`i − 1`); expectations of nonnegative quantities are lower Lebesgue integrals. The copies are built
from any functional representations `RX`, `Rα` of `X` and `α` (Lemma 2.1 gives one; two give
almost surely equal copies). `C` may depend on everything except `N` and `i`. -/
theorem theorem_2_23 {n k d : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setup n d Ω)
    (D : Data n k d) (V : Derivs n k d) (T : ℝ≥0) (hT : 0 < T)
    (h17 : Assumption_2_17 D V) (h19 : Assumption_2_19 D) (h20 : Assumption_2_20 D)
    (m : ℝ≥0 → Ω → P2 n) (α : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k) (X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n) (hMFE : IsMFE S D T m α X)
    (αN : ∀ N : ℕ, Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k) (XN : ∀ N : ℕ, Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n)
    (hNash : ∀ N, 0 < N → IsNashN S D T N (αN N) (XN N))
    (RX : ContRep S T X) (Rα : ProgRep S T α) :
    (∃ C : ℝ, ∀ N : ℕ, 0 < N → ∀ i : Fin N,
      ∫⁻ ω, ⨆ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, ‖XN N i t ω - RX.copy i t ω‖ₑ ^ 2 ∂S.P ≤
        ENNReal.ofReal (C / N)) ∧
    (AlphaHatReg D → ∃ C : ℝ, ∀ N : ℕ, 0 < N → ∀ i : Fin N,
      ∫⁻ ω, ∫⁻ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T,
          ‖Rα.copy i s.toNNReal ω - αN N i s.toNNReal ω‖ₑ ^ 2 ∂volume ∂S.P ≤
        ENNReal.ofReal (C / N)) := by sorry

end CtrlVolMFG
